e? Which representations reveal those relationships? Which words are indispensable? This prevents the common failure in which a short text becomes a pile of isolated definitions. A resource is simple to navigate when it makes relationships visible and decisions predictable, even if the science remains appropriately demanding. For the opening lesson, a blank body map can elicit evidence of prior knowledge without requiring a permanent track. Ask students what they believe happens to a familiar food after it enters the body. Use the responses to identify missing links, competing models and language needs. The diagnostic is not a ranking ritual. It is routing information. For the first core explanation, choose a causal chain short enough to hold together but rich enough to be scientifically meaningful. Introduce a small number of indispensable terms before asking students to coordinate them. Keep the same colours, arrows and definitions across teacher presentation, student material and assessment. Six experiments using engineering and biology materials found that integrating mutually dependent sources helped when learners would otherwise have to combine them mentally, but not when each source could be understood alone. [6] At the checkpoint, use one or two questions that distinguish different difficulties. A student who cannot define absorption needs a different support from a student who knows the definition but cannot explain why surface area matters. Routing by the observed error is a more instructionally specific heuristic than assigning a fixed identity such as \"weak.\" Reviews of scaffolding emphasise this contingency: support should respond to current performance and later fade. [13] After the core is secure, choice can widen. Students can apply the shared model to protein, fat or carbohydrate, compare two dietary claims, explain a graph or evaluate a misleading diagram. The sequence moves from common orientation toward increasing independence. The important variable is not whether the student experiences choice, but what intellectual work remains common across the options."
