 whether the entire unit was good, the teacher can ask which layer failed. If students reached the correct page but misunderstood digestion, the core explanation or instruction needs repair. If they understood orally but could not enter the text, the access layer needs repair. If confident students completed repetitive work without learning, the routing or depth layer needs repair. If everybody asked which document to open, the architecture failed before the science began. Such diagnosis protects teacher energy. It turns a vague pressure to differentiate everything into a finite set of design decisions. Research on differentiated instruction records both perceived benefits and stress, while not directly comparing these material architectures. [17] The promised workload reduction is therefore not an established effect. It is a testable local expectation: shared components should reduce duplicated maintenance, while targeted layers should concentrate effort where it changes access or learning. The first unit is consequently allowed to be incomplete. It must be coherent, accessible enough to begin and honest about what is not yet known. After teaching, the teacher can keep the layers that solved recurring problems, fold widely needed support into the core and discard the rest. The architecture grows like a path worn by actual footsteps, not like a road network drawn before anyone has entered the landscape."
