          "text": "The recommendation is stronger than intuition but weaker than a direct comparative trial. Much cognitive load research comes from mathematics, multimedia and higher education. Studies of differentiation use inconsistent definitions and often researcher-made measures. Choice experiments usually manipulate one bounded decision rather than the navigation of an entire unit. Teacher workload studies are largely observational, self-reported or practice-based. Consequently, no exact number of materials can be scientifically prescribed. â€œAt most two choicesâ€ is a useful restraint in some novice situations, not a law. â€œOne common coreâ€ is a governance principle, not proof that every student should receive identical media. â€œLess cognitive loadâ€ is not automatically a virtue if it is achieved by removing the relationships students need to understand. The synthesis cautions against two extremes. A single undifferentiated text should not be assumed to meet every access need, and a large open menu should not be assumed to constitute effective differentiation. Between those extremes lies a disciplined design: common intellectual direction, multiple purposeful representations, responsive supports and a teacher who remains the architect of the route. The goal is not to give every student a different road. It is to make the important road legible, then place the right handrail where someone actually needs it. For the first unit, the most responsible decision is therefore modest. Build one strong route. Add only the supports whose functions can be named. Preserve the full scientific story. Watch how students enter, navigate, remember and explain. Watch how much time the system demands from its teacher. Then revise once, from evidence rather than anxiety."
