        {
          "ankare": "",
          "rubrik": "Ingress",
          "text": "It is tempting to see four kinds of student. That temptation is the first problem this article tries to solve. Passenger, Achiever, Resister and Explorer are more useful as names for temporary classroom modes. The same student may move between them during one lesson. A pupil may explore confidently in practical science, resist a writing task, become a passenger during a long explanation, and act as an achiever when a familiar test format appears. The names should therefore direct the teacher's attention towards the present relationship between support, purpose and responsibility. They should never become identities, diagnoses or seating plans. The deeper question is not whether teachers should choose structure or agency. It is how structure can make agency possible without quietly replacing it. Students need access to knowledge, examples, language, tools and feedback. They also need chances to judge, choose, monitor, revise and take responsibility. If support is removed too early, independence can become abandonment. If support remains after it has served its purpose, teaching can produce accurate compliance without intellectual ownership. Research does not provide a simple formula for finding the perfect balance. The evidence reviewed for this article is both useful and limited. It offers a bounded picture of when problem solving before instruction has helped, when it has not, and how teacher autonomy support is associated with several forms of student motivation. It does not validate the four modes. It does not prove a causal mechanism joining structure to agency. It does not tell a Swedish lower secondary teacher exactly how much support a particular pupil needs on Tuesday morning. The initial research question was framed partly through cognitive load theory. The final evidence set did not retain a directly verified cognitive load theory source. This article therefore does not use that theory to explain motivation, agency or the four modes. Questions about task complexity and prior knowledge remain important, but they are not presented here as verified cognitive load theory claims. That limitation is not a reason to retreat into intuition. It is a reason to use evidence with greater precision. The aim here is to build a practical discipline for teaching under uncertainty: diagnose tentatively, support narrowly, and transfer responsibility deliberately."
