        {
          "ankare": "the-false-choice-between-structure-and-freedom",
          "rubrik": "The false choice between structure and freedom",
          "text": "Discussions about teaching often become polarised around an unhelpful pair of images. In one, the teacher explains clearly, models the task and directs attention. Students are protected from confusion, but they risk becoming passive. In the other, students explore, choose and construct solutions. They exercise initiative, but some may lack the knowledge or orientation needed to make the exploration productive. Real classrooms do not fit either image for long. A lesson is a sequence of changing relationships. At one moment, the teacher knows where attention needs to go and the student does not. At another, the student has enough orientation to make a meaningful decision. Later, a misconception becomes visible and the teacher intervenes. Then the student tries again. Good teaching is not located at one fixed point between control and freedom. It consists partly in changing the relationship at the right moments. This is why the key unit of thought should be a transfer of responsibility, not a teaching method in isolation. Responsibility has several parts. Who identifies the goal? Who selects a strategy? Who decides whether the current approach is working? Who notices an error? Who chooses when to ask for help? Who explains the quality criteria? Who decides whether a solution is finished? A teacher can retain responsibility for one part while transferring another. Students can choose a method within a clearly defined scientific question. They can evaluate competing explanations after the teacher has supplied essential concepts. They can plan a practical investigation while safety constraints remain non negotiable. The central proposition of this article is therefore pedagogical, not causal: structure can support agency when it gives students better resources for consequential judgment. Structure can also reduce opportunities for agency when it takes over judgments that students could productively make themselves. The same worksheet, model answer or guiding question may help in one moment and obstruct in another. This proposition is deliberately modest. The evidence base reviewed here does not directly establish it as a general law. It is an organising argument that must survive contact with research, classroom observation and counterexamples."
