        {
          "ankare": "what-the-sequencing-evidence-actually-says",
          "rubrik": "What the sequencing evidence actually says",
          "text": "One common version of the structure and agency debate concerns sequence. Should students receive instruction before they attempt a problem, or should they first encounter a problem that exposes the limits of their current thinking? A meta analysis by Sinha and Kapur compared problem solving followed by instruction with instruction followed by problem solving. Across 53 studies, the synthesis found an overall advantage for problem solving followed by instruction on selected conceptual knowledge and transfer outcomes [1] . This is important evidence against the claim that instruction must always come first. It is not evidence that problem solving should always come first. The same review reported that the advantage was outcome specific. The procedural knowledge analysis produced a pooled estimate close to zero [1] . The reviewed claim is therefore narrower: conceptual knowledge and transfer showed an overall advantage in the synthesis, while procedural knowledge did not show the same general pattern. A nonsignificant pooled estimate does not establish that the sequences are equivalent in every classroom or for every procedural outcome. The review also has boundaries that matter for teachers. Most comparisons concerned mathematics or basic sciences. Studies varied in population, implementation, fidelity and measurement. Candidate studies were identified through citation searching seeded by five Productive Failure papers and checked against a previous qualitative review, rather than through a broad independent search across many databases. One of the authors developed the Productive Failure approach, which does not invalidate the work but makes author allegiance a relevant source consideration. The pooled result was heterogeneous. It should not be converted into a universal rule. A primary study by Loibl and colleagues sharpens that caution [2] . The experiment involved Dutch secondary mathematics students and compared problem solving before instruction with instruction before problem solving. It also varied whether students worked with contrasting cases. The conceptual posttest showed no statistically significant main effect of phase order. That null result does not prove equivalence. The conceptual measure had low reliability, overall scores were low, instruction consisted of a brief recorded video, and the study covered one mathematics topic in two academically oriented Dutch school tracks. The procedural result pointed in another direction. On an immediate two item procedural fluency measure, students who received instruction before problem solving scored higher than students who solved problems before instruction [2] . This was one bounded experiment, with one topic, a short instructional treatment and an immediate outcome. It does not overturn the meta analysis. It shows why the outcome, timing, population and implementation must remain attached to every sequence claim. Two further studies by Nachtigall and colleagues examined productive failure beyond STEM domains [3] . Both took place in a university out of school laboratory with German tenth grade classes studying social science research methods. Whole classes, rather than individual students, were assigned to conditions. Both groups eventually received explanation. The difference concerned whether students attempted the problem before that explanation or received explanation first and then practised. The first study showed a negative pattern for the problem first condition on the measured learning outcomes. The second showed a null pattern. They should not be collapsed into two harmful effects. Nor do they show that explanation was absent from productive failure. The defensible synthesis across the reviewed sources is this: Across these sources, placing problem solving before instruction did not uniformly improve the measured learning outcomes. That sentence is less dramatic than either slogan in the debate. It is also more useful. It directs the teacher towards four questions: What kind of learning outcome matters here? What knowledge and strategies do students bring to the task? What happens after the initial attempt? What will the teacher do with the information revealed by students' attempts? The phrase productive struggle can otherwise hide several different events. A student may compare ideas, notice a contradiction and reorganise a model. A student may also repeat an unproductive strategy, wait for rescue, or become occupied with avoiding embarrassment. The presence of difficulty does not tell us which event is occurring. The reviewed sources do not show that placing problem solving before later instruction will by itself create a valuable intellectual process. This leads to a practical distinction. A problem can come before a full explanation without coming before all orientation. The teacher may establish the question, define essential terms, show how materials are used, make the success criteria visible, and provide a way to record partial ideas. Students can then attempt, compare and revise. The relevant choice is not simply explanation or no explanation. It is which responsibilities are held by the teacher now, which are given to students now, and what information the next phase should use."
