        {
          "ankare": "motivation-changes-the-meaning-of-support",
          "rubrik": "Motivation changes the meaning of support",
          "text": "Sequence evidence tells only part of the story. A student can complete a well structured task without experiencing ownership. Another can welcome the same structure because it makes meaningful participation possible. To understand the difference, teachers need a motivational lens as well as an instructional one. Bureau and colleagues synthesised research on antecedents of autonomous and controlled forms of motivation [4] . In their bivariate meta analyses, measured teacher autonomy support was positively associated with intrinsic motivation and identified motivation, and negatively associated with amotivation. These were reliability corrected correlations across varied educational levels, countries, reporters, measures and contexts. They were not causal effects. The distinction matters. The synthesis does not show that a particular autonomy supportive move will cause a particular student to become intrinsically motivated. Heterogeneity was very high. Publication bias analyses suggested that the unadjusted associations for intrinsic and identified motivation may have been overstated. Even with those limitations, the pattern supplies a useful boundary. Motivation cannot be treated as a decorative extra added after the instructional design is complete. How students experience purpose, choice and ownership is related to the quality of their motivation. A teacher who wants students to accept increasing responsibility must pay attention to this relationship. Autonomy support is not the same thing as leaving students alone. In this article, the term is used cautiously to name practices that make room for meaningful student perspective and volition. It does not imply unlimited choice. It does not remove curricular aims, safety rules, deadlines or quality criteria. A choice between two equally meaningless tasks does not create agency. Neither does asking students to select a method when they cannot yet understand the consequences of that selection. The practical question is whether a choice carries intelligible consequences. Students may choose which evidence to present, which model to test, which misconception to investigate, or which representation best communicates an explanation. The teacher can preserve the learning goal while varying the route. In other moments, the route itself is the object of learning and should not be chosen too quickly. A student learning to design a controlled investigation needs eventually to decide which variables matter. If the teacher always supplies the design, the student can complete the practical work without learning to make that judgment. Agency therefore requires more than activity. It involves authorship of consequential decisions. It also requires the ability to revise those decisions in response to evidence and criteria."
