          "text": "Teachers must make decisions before they possess complete explanations. Waiting for certainty is not an option. The solution is not to stop diagnosing, but to diagnose tentatively. A tentative diagnosis has three features. First, it stays close to observation. â€œShe has not started after three minutes and has reread the first instruction twiceâ€ is more useful than â€œShe is unmotivated.â€ â€œHe selected the familiar procedure without comparing alternativesâ€ is more useful than â€œHe is an Achiever.â€ Observation preserves possibilities that labels close. Second, it generates more than one plausible explanation. A student who is silent may be disengaged, uncertain, processing, translating, monitoring the social situation, or preparing a response. An accessibility barrier may resemble disengagement. Processing latency may resemble disengagement. Public verbal initiative is not the only acceptable expression of agency. These are pedagogical safeguards, not claims that the present evidence package has established a cross field causal model. Their purpose is ethical and practical. A teacher should avoid converting ambiguous behaviour into a stable account of character. Third, a tentative diagnosis leads to the smallest useful change. Change one feature of support when possible. Clarify the purpose but keep the task. Reduce public exposure but keep the reasoning demand. Provide a worked starting point but leave the comparison open. Offer two representations but retain the same criterion. The response to that change is information, not proof. If participation improves after the purpose is clarified, lack of purpose may have contributed. The change does not prove that purpose was the only cause. It certainly does not establish a pupil's readiness level or mode. This distinction protects against a seductive classroom error: treating every successful intervention as confirmation of the story that motivated it. A student may respond for reasons the teacher did not anticipate. The probe should improve the next decision without pretending to reveal the whole learner."
