          "text": "Support is easiest to remove when it has a clearly defined job. Broad support tends to become the environment. Narrow support can be inspected, adjusted and transferred. Consider a science task in which students must explain why a sealed syringe becomes harder to compress. The teacher could provide the question, the equipment, a safety boundary, a particle representation and a complete explanation template. Students might produce correct work. It would be difficult to know which judgments they could make independently. A narrower design assigns different jobs to different supports. The equipment boundary protects safety. A short demonstration ensures that everyone can operate the syringe. A diagram makes an invisible model available for discussion. A comparison question directs attention towards the relationship between volume and particle spacing. A criterion states that the explanation must connect observation to model. The teacher can then decide which element should be transferred. Perhaps students choose the most informative observation. Perhaps they create the diagram. Perhaps they compare two explanations, one of which confuses particle size with spacing. The task remains structured, but student judgment has a visible place. This also makes fading more honest, even though this article makes no general research claim about a universal fading sequence. A teacher can say what is being removed and why. â€œYesterday I supplied the comparison table. Today your group will decide which measurements belong in it.â€ â€œI will still give you the safety constraints, but you will design the order of trials.â€ â€œThe criterion remains the same, but I will not mark the point where your explanation becomes inconsistent.â€ Narrow support helps students notice the changing contract. It helps the teacher notice whether responsibility has actually moved."
