          "text": "Responsibility does not transfer merely because the teacher becomes quiet. Silence can leave the original authority structure intact. Students may continue to guess what the teacher wants, only with less information. A deliberate transfer names the decision, provides the resources needed to make it, and creates a later occasion for evaluation. For example: â€œYou will decide which model best explains the evidence. I will make sure the three models are understood. Your group must record one reason for rejecting each model. At the checkpoint, you may revise your choice.â€ The teacher still structures the intellectual space. Students own a consequential judgment within it. This approach avoids two weak versions of choice. The first is cosmetic choice, such as selecting a font, colour or order of presentation when those decisions have little bearing on the learning. The second is premature strategic choice, where students are asked to select a method before they can understand the method's consequences. Meaningful agency is not measured by the number of choices. One serious judgment can matter more than ten decorative options. Deliberate transfer also requires return. Students need to see what happened because of their choice. A prediction is compared with data. A model is revised. A plan is evaluated against criteria. Without return, choice can become expression without learning. The return phase is also where the teacher's expertise becomes visible without cancelling student authorship. The teacher can name a pattern, introduce a concept or demonstrate a more powerful representation after students have produced material that gives the explanation a purpose. The sequence evidence reviewed earlier suggests that such problem first designs can benefit selected conceptual and transfer outcomes under some conditions [1] . The same evidence also warns that they do not uniformly improve every outcome [1] [2] [3] . The question is not whether students discovered the canonical answer unaided. It is whether their initial work created useful contrasts, questions or commitments that the next phase could develop."
