          "ankare": "the-bounded-role-of-ai",
          "rubrik": "The bounded role of AI",
          "text": "Generative AI can easily intensify the central problem of this article. It can supply explanations, plans, feedback and language so quickly that a learner appears productive while important judgments have moved out of view. It can also provide alternative representations or low exposure opportunities for rehearsal. This research package establishes no causal claim about generative AI in Swedish lower secondary teaching. The present section is therefore a design boundary, not an evidence review. The key question is not whether AI is allowed. It is which responsibility the tool is taking. If an AI system generates the research question, selects the evidence, writes the explanation and evaluates the result, the student's activity may be reduced to acceptance. If the student supplies a claim, asks the system for two counterarguments, checks them against sources and records why one was rejected, the tool may support a consequential judgment that remains with the student. A teacher can apply the same three disciplines used elsewhere in the article. Diagnose tentatively. A polished AI assisted answer does not reveal which parts the student understands. Ask for a decision history, a rejected alternative or an oral explanation of one connection. Support narrowly. Give the tool one defined job. It might clarify a term, propose a counterexample, translate a question for access, or format observations. Avoid allowing it to absorb the entire intellectual sequence. Protect sustained reading and writing. A summary may support access, but if it becomes the only route, the student no longer practises holding an extended argument in mind. Decide which text must be read in full, what notes the student must make, and which synthesis they must write before asking AI to compress or reformulate it. Transfer deliberately. State what the student must still decide and how that decision will be evaluated. The student might choose which generated explanation best fits the evidence and identify an unsupported sentence. AI should not become the framework running through the whole lesson. It is one possible support whose job must be visible. When its job cannot be named, it is difficult to know what responsibility remains with the learner."
