          "text": "The reviewed evidence supports a focused set of statements. It also leaves large gaps. It does not establish Passenger, Achiever, Resister and Explorer as valid psychological types. The modes are situational questions created for pedagogical reflection. It does not establish a universal order of problem solving and instruction. A synthesis found benefits for selected conceptual and transfer outcomes, but not the same pattern for procedural knowledge [1] . A bounded secondary mathematics experiment found no conceptual advantage and an immediate procedural advantage for instruction first [2] . Two non STEM studies supplied negative and null counterexamples under particular conditions [3] . It does not establish that autonomy support causes motivation. The reviewed meta analysis reported associations across heterogeneous and mainly correlational evidence [4] . It does not establish that clear structure causes agency. That connection remains the organising pedagogical argument of this article, not a verified causal claim. It does not establish a formula for adapting support to individual readiness. The S18 classroom scaffolding source could not be inspected in the present environment and was excluded. No evidence from that source enters the article. It does not establish that visible behaviour reveals its cause. The recommendation to generate competing explanations is a safeguard for teacher judgment. It does not establish effects of generative AI. The AI section contains bounded design questions only. These exclusions make the article less encyclopaedic. They also make it more trustworthy. A useful teaching argument should show where research ends and professional judgment begins."
