        {
          "ankare": "the-reversal",
          "rubrik": "The reversal",
          "text": "Imagine preparing a region so that no detector there registers a particle excitation. The intuitive picture says that nothing remains. Quantum field theory gives a stranger answer: the state can still possess vacuum correlations, and field variables remain part of the theory because particles are not its basic starting objects. Particles are particular, and sometimes only approximate, ways in which field states carry energy, momentum, spin, charge and other quantum numbers. [4] [5] This reversal is the conceptual doorway. Do not begin with four forces and ask which particle carries each one. Begin with fields, quantum states and couplings. The familiar catalogue of particles and forces then becomes a set of consequences. There is one more reversal to make. A quantum field is not quite a substance spread through space, like fog or water. That metaphor helps briefly, then fails. In common formulations a quantum field is represented by an operator valued distribution over spacetime. After smearing with test functions, it helps construct operators associated with finite spacetime regions. In algebraic formulations, algebras of observables are assigned directly to regions. These structures belong to a framework that predicts probabilities for possible measurement outcomes. Whether the field should also be regarded as literally what exists is an ontological interpretation, strongly motivated by the success and structure of the theory, but not separately photographed by an experiment. That distinction matters. Quantum field theory is experimentally extraordinary. A simple mental image of quantum fields is not itself an experimental result."
