          "ankare": "why-fields-enter-the-story-at-all",
          "rubrik": "Why fields enter the story at all",
          "text": "Quantum mechanics is often introduced as a theory of a fixed number of particles. One writes a wave function for one electron, then perhaps a more complicated wave function for two. That framework works beautifully when changes in particle number can be neglected. A fundamental local relativistic theory must, however, accommodate processes in which particle number changes. [1] [4] [5] Collision energy can produce massive particle excitations when the conservation laws and interaction dynamics allow it. Particles can also annihilate and transfer their energy into other excitations. A theory with a permanently fixed number of elementary objects is therefore too small for a fundamental local account of relativistic interactions. Quantum field theory solves this by changing the question. Instead of quantising the motion of a predetermined collection of particles, it quantises systems that can support zero, one, two or any allowed number of particle excitations. Creation and annihilation are then changes of state, not exceptions added after the fact. There is a second reason. Relativity demands a local account of influence. What happens in one region must not allow controllable information to jump instantaneously to a spacelike separated region. Quantum fields provide local observables while allowing quantum states to possess correlations across distance. This combination of local causal dynamics and nonclassical correlation is one of the deepest structural achievements of the theory. So fields are not introduced merely because physicists enjoy continuous mathematics. They provide a framework in which quantum theory, changing particle number and relativistic locality coexist."
