        {
          "ankare": "first-step-what-physicists-mean-by-a-field",
          "rubrik": "First step: what physicists mean by a field",
          "text": "A classical field assigns something to every location and time. A temperature field assigns a number. The electromagnetic field assigns electric and magnetic vectors. The gravitational field in general relativity is encoded in spacetime geometry. A quantum field is not merely a classical field with small random wiggles. Quantum theory changes what counts as a physical state and what questions can have simultaneous definite answers. In operator formulations, fields enter as operator valued distributions acting on quantum states. The state determines probabilities for possible outcomes. Appropriately smeared, gauge invariant self adjoint combinations can represent local observables, while other field variables construct states and correlation functions without being separately measurable. This yields three ideas that should be kept separate: The field type tells us which kind of quantum degree of freedom the model contains, such as an electron field or electromagnetic field. The quantum state tells us the condition of all those fields, such as vacuum, one particle, many particles or an entangled state. A particle is a quantised excitation carrying characteristic quantum numbers, with energy and momentum either definite in an idealised mode or distributed across a wave packet. The concept is cleanest for free fields and for particles sufficiently far before or after an interaction. The last qualification is important. Inside a strongly interacting system, or in curved spacetime, the concept of a particle can become approximate or observer dependent. The field description is more general than the particle description."
