          "text": "A free bosonic field can be decomposed mathematically into modes. Each mode behaves like a quantum harmonic oscillator. A classical oscillator can have any energy. A quantum oscillator has discrete excitation levels. Moving from one level to the next changes the energy by a quantum. In a field, those quanta have the properties we associate with particles. [1] [4] [6] For one bosonic mode the energy levels take the form E n = â„ Ï‰ ( n + 1 2 ) . E_n=\\hbar\\omega\\left(n+\\frac{1}{2}\\right). The integer n n counts mode excitations. The zero point term does not mean that n = 0 n=0 contains half a detectable particle. It expresses the ground state energy of the oscillator representation. In nongravitational calculations only appropriately defined differences and renormalised quantities enter observations. Coupling vacuum energy consistently to gravity leads into the separate cosmological constant problem. Fermion fields, including electron and quark fields, are quantised differently. Their creation and annihilation operators anticommute, and a given single particle state cannot be occupied by two identical fermions. This is the field theoretic origin of Fermi statistics and the Pauli exclusion principle. Bosonic modes allow repeated occupation. Fermionic modes do not. For the electromagnetic field, a quantum is a photon. For the electron field, an excitation is an electron, while the corresponding antiparticle excitation is a positron. This is why quantum field theory naturally accommodates particle creation and annihilation. Energy can move between fields and change which particle excitations are present, while the theory preserves the relevant conservation laws. In the standard inertial flat spacetime description, the vacuum is the lowest energy state of the fields, not a classical void. It has quantum correlations and nonzero fluctuations of suitable observables. This does not mean that the vacuum is full of short lived ordinary particles constantly appearing in a directly observable way. That popular phrase confuses a calculational representation with a detector level event."
