          "text": "We can now state more precisely what was only promised at the beginning: quantum field theory combines local observable dynamics with globally nonseparable quantum states. [5] Relativistic quantum field theory combines two statements that initially seem incompatible. First, operations in spacelike separated regions cannot be used to send information faster than light. In formal language, suitable gauge invariant local observables commute at spacelike separation. This condition is called microcausality. Second, the quantum state can contain correlations between those regions that no classical local model can reproduce. Entanglement does not carry a controllable message by itself, so it does not violate relativistic causality. A useful mental distinction is between dynamics and state . The dynamics is local. The state need not factorise into independent pieces for every region. The theory can therefore satisfy microcausality and prohibit faster than light signalling without making the world a collection of locally self contained classical parts. This matters for understanding fields. A field is not merely a value stored independently at each point. The physically relevant object includes patterns of correlation between possible local measurements."
