          "text": "In classical mechanics, a force changes the motion of an object. In quantum field theory the deeper description is an interaction term, or coupling, in the theory. It specifies how states of one or more fields can influence one another and which transformations are allowed. Much of that information is compressed into an action, S = âˆ« d 4 x â„’ . S=\\int d^4x\\,\\mathcal{L}. The Lagrangian density â„’ \\mathcal{L} identifies the fields, their free propagation and their permitted couplings. This expression is not a visual explanation, but it is the formal centre of the subject. To understand a quantum field theory is, in large part, to understand which terms may occur in â„’ \\mathcal{L} , which symmetries constrain them and how their predictions depend on energy scale. For example, the electron field couples to the electromagnetic field because the electron carries electric charge. That coupling permits processes in which an electron emits or absorbs a photon. It also produces the electromagnetic interaction between charged systems. The simplest schematic example is a product of fields in the Lagrangian. In quantum electrodyna×­µçkh‘éì¶»§q«^t…¹MÑ…Ñ¥ÍÑ¥…°5…¹Õ…°½˜5•¹Ñ…°¥Í½É‘•ÉÌ€¡M4¤èM4µ%$€ ÄäØà¤è1¥ÍÑ•p‰!åÁ•É­¥¹•Ñ¥ŒI•…Ñ¥½¸½˜¡¥±‘¡½½±pˆ™½ÕÍ¥¹œ•á±ÕÍ¥Ù•±ä½¸µ½Ñ½ÈÉ•ÍÑ±•ÍÍ¹•ÍÌ…¹…ÍÍÕµ¥¹œÑ¡”½¹‘¥Ñ¥½¸¹…ÑÕÉ…±±äÙ…¹¥Í¡•…ĞÁÕ‰•ÉÑä¸M4µ%%$€ ÄäàÀ¤èİ…Ñ•ÉÍ¡•µ½µ•¹Ğ±•‰äÈ¸Y¥É¥¹¥„½Õ±…Ì¸Q¡”µ…¹Õ…°¥¹ÑÉ½‘Õ•ÑÑ•¹Ñ¥½¸•™¥¥Ğ¥Í½É‘•È€¡¤€°•áÁ±¥¥Ñ±äÉ•½¹¥é¥¹œ™½ÈÑ¡”™¥ÉÍĞÑ¥µ”Ñ¡…ĞÍ•Ù•É”¥¹…ÑÑ•¹Ñ¥½¸½Õ±•á¥ÍĞİ¥Ñ¡½ÕĞµ½Ñ½È¡åÁ•É…Ñ¥Ù¥Ñä¸M4µ%X€ ÄääĞ¤€˜M4´ÔµQH€ ÈÀÈÈ¤èI”µÕ¹¥™¥•Ñ¡”Õµ‰É•±±„Õ¹‘•È!€°•ÍÑ…‰±¥Í¡¥¹œÑ¡É•”‘¥ÍÑ¥¹ĞÁÉ•Í•¹Ñ…Ñ¥½¹ÌèAÉ•‘½µ¥¹…¹Ñ±ä%¹…ÑÑ•¹Ñ¥Ù”€°AÉ•‘½µ¥¹…¹Ñ±ä!åÁ•É…Ñ¥Ù”µ%µÁÕ±Í¥Ù”€°…¹½µ‰¥¹•lÄÉt€¸Q¡”M4´Ô…±Í¼É…¥Í•Ñ¡”½¹Í•Ğ…”É¥Ñ•É¥½¸™É½´€ÜÑ¼€ÄÈ…¹™½Éµ…±±ä•ÍÑ…‰±¥Í¡•É¥Ñ•É¥„™½È…‘Õ±Ğ‘¥…¹½Í•Ì¸ˆ(€€€€€€€ô°(€€€€€€€ì(€€€€€€€€€€‰…¹­…É”ˆè€ˆˆ°(€€€€€€€€€€‰ÉÕ‰É¥¬ˆè